Title: Uncle Vanya, Act 4 (the morphine)
Author: Anton Chekhov
Source: Project Gutenberg ebook 1756, lines 1930 to 2010; translated by Marian Fell

INT. VOITSKI'S ROOM 1 - DAY

ASTROFF
What nonsense! What sort of a new life can you and I look forward to? We can have no hope.

!Crossly

VOITSKI
None?

ASTROFF
None. Of that I am convinced.

VOITSKI
Tell me what to do. I feel such a burning pain here.

!He puts his hand to his heart

ASTROFF
Stop! It may be that posterity, which will despise us for our blind and stupid lives, will find some road to happiness; but we--you and I--have but one hope, the hope that we may be visited by visions, perhaps by pleasant ones, as we lie resting in our graves. Yes, brother, there were only two respectable, intelligent men in this county, you and I. Ten years or so of this life of ours, this miserable life, have sucked us under, and we have become as contemptible and petty as the rest. But don't try to talk me out of my purpose! Give me what you took from me, will you?

!Shouts angrily

!Then, more gently

!Sighing

VOITSKI
I took nothing from you.

INT. VOITSKI'S ROOM 2 - DAY

ASTROFF
You took a little bottle of morphine out of my medicine-case. Listen! If you are positively determined to make an end to yourself, go into the woods and shoot yourself there. Give up the morphine, or there will be a lot of talk and guesswork; people will think I gave it to you. I don't fancy having to perform a post-mortem on you. Do you think I should find it interesting?

!A pause

!SONIA comes in.

VOITSKI
Leave me alone.

ASTROFF
Sonia, your uncle has stolen a bottle of morphine out of my medicine-case and won't give it up. Tell him that his behaviour is--well, unwise. I haven't time, I must be going.

!To SONIA

SONIA
Uncle Vanya, did you take the morphine?

ASTROFF
Yes, he took it. I am absolutely sure.

!A pause

SONIA
Give it up! Why do you want to frighten us? Give it up, Uncle Vanya! My misfortune is perhaps even greater than yours, but I am not plunged in despair. I endure my sorrow, and shall endure it until my life comes to a natural end. You must endure yours, too. Give it up! Dear, darling Uncle Vanya. Give it up! You are so good, I am sure you will have pity on us and give it up. You must endure your sorrow, Uncle Vanya; you must endure it.

!Tenderly

!A pause

!She weeps

!VOITSKI takes a bottle from the drawer of the table and hands it to ASTROFF.

INT. VOITSKI'S ROOM 3 - DAY

VOITSKI
There it is! And now, we must get to work at once; we must do something, or else I shall not be able to endure it.

!To SONIA

SONIA
Yes, yes, to work! As soon as we have seen them off we shall go to work. Everything is in a muddle!

!She nervously straightens out the papers on the table

ASTROFF
Now I can be off.

!Putting the bottle in his case, which he straps together

INT. VOITSKI'S ROOM 4 - DAY

!HELENA comes in.

HELENA
Are you here, Ivan? We are starting in a moment. Go to Alexander, he wants to speak to you.

SONIA
Go, Uncle Vanya. Come, you and papa must make peace; that is absolutely necessary.

!She takes VOITSKI 'S arm

!SONIA and VOITSKI go out.

INT. VOITSKI'S ROOM 5 - DAY

HELENA
I am going away. Good-bye.

!She gives ASTROFF her hand

ASTROFF
So soon?

HELENA
The carriage is waiting.

ASTROFF
Good-bye.

HELENA
You promised me you would go away yourself to-day.
